    id: "ako",
    title: "I, you, he, she: ako, ikaw, siya and ang / si",
    blurb: "Say who someone is (a teacher, a student, an American) with ako, ikaw, siya, and the markers si and ang.",
    deck: "About me",
    body: `
<p>English says "I am a teacher": the person, then "am", then the main word. Cebuano has no word for "am, is, are", and it turns the order round: <b>Maestro ko</b>, literally "teacher I".</p>
<h3>I, you, he and she</h3>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>English</th><th>Full form</th><th>Short form</th></tr><tr><td>I</td><td class="k">ako</td><td class="k">ko</td></tr><tr><td>you (one person)</td><td class="k">ikaw</td><td class="k">ka</td></tr><tr><td>he, she</td><td class="k">siya</td><td>none</td></tr></table></div>
<p><b>siya</b> means both "he" and "she": Cebuano does not show which. Context tells you, so a sentence about your sister and one about your brother look exactly the same.</p>
<h3>The main word comes first</h3>
<p>Start with what you are saying about the person (a job, a nationality, a describing word), then say who.</p>
{{ex:Maestro ko.|mah-ehs-troh koh|I'm a teacher.}}
{{ex:Amerikana siya.|ah-meh-ree-kah-nah see-YAH|She's American.}}
{{ex:Estudyante ka.|ehs-too-jahn-teh kah|You're a student.}}
<p>Words for jobs and nationalities from Spanish change for a woman: <b>maestro</b> for a man, <b>maestra</b> for a woman; <b>Amerikano</b>, <b>Amerikana</b>.</p>
<h3>Short or full?</h3>
<p>After the main word you can use either form: <b>Maestro ko</b> and <b>Maestro ako</b> both mean "I'm a teacher"; the short one is more common. But <b>ko</b> and <b>ka</b> can never start a sentence. To start with "I" or "you", use the full form, as you will see next lesson in <b>Ako si Emma</b>.</p>
<h3>si before names, ang before other nouns</h3>
<p>The person or thing the sentence is about needs a small marker in front. Names take <b>si</b>. Other nouns take <b>ang</b>, which is close to "the". English has nothing like <b>si</b>, so it is easy to forget.</p>
{{ex:Maestra si Ana.|mah-ehs-trah see ah-nah|Ana is a teacher.}}
{{ex:Amerikano si Ben.|ah-meh-ree-kah-noh see behn|Ben is American.}}
{{ex:Estudyante ang bata.|ehs-too-jahn-teh ahng BAH-tahq|The child is a student.}}
<p>The word with <b>si</b> or <b>ang</b> is the focus of the sentence, and it comes after the main word, just like <b>ko</b>, <b>ka</b> and <b>siya</b>.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> English order with a name and no marker. <b>Ana maestra.</b> → <b>Maestra si Ana.</b></div>
`,
